1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This is Task 4, Git and GitHub repository initialization.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
A repository is the project folder plus its complete history, stored in a hidden dot git folder.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Here I list the Git related items in the project root: the dot git folder, the dot github folder, dot gitattributes, dot gitignore, CONTRIBUTING, README and a VERSION file.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Each one has a purpose, and I will open them.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The README is the front page of the project.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It says what the project is, what the features are, and links to the documentation.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Anyone who opens the repository on GitHub reads this first.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
It also contains the commands to run and test the application.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The dot gitignore file lists files that Git must never store: the virtual environment, the database, test reports, environment files with passwords, and certificates.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Here are the real rules.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Then I ask Git itself, with check ignore, why three files are ignored.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
It names the rule and the line number.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
That is how secrets like the Jenkins password file stay out of the repository.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The dot gitattributes file tells Git how to treat text files.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Windows uses a different line ending than Linux.

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
The first rule stores everything with Linux line endings in the repository.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
That matters because our Dockerfiles, Ansible files and scripts run inside Linux containers, and Windows line endings would break them.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
CONTRIBUTING documents the team conventions.

19
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Main holds released code.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Develop is where features meet and are tested together.

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Every change is made on its own feature branch, cut from develop.

22
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Branch names are lowercase with hyphens, and experiment branches are never merged.

23
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Writing the rules down means everyone works the same way.

24
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
These are the issue templates in the dot github folder.

25
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:28,000
A bug report template and a feature request template give every issue the same structure: what happened, what was expected, and acceptance criteria.

26
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
GitHub shows them automatically when someone clicks New issue.

27
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
This is the pull request template.

28
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
When a developer opens a pull request, GitHub pre fills this text.

29
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:56,000
It asks what the change does and how it was tested, and gives an author checklist: branch name, commit message, linter clean, tests passing, Jenkins green and no secrets.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
A reviewer has a second checklist below.

31
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
This is what supports code review.

32
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Now the real repository.

33
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Git status shows the current branch and whether anything is changed.

34
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
A branch is a separate line of development.

35
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Below is the list of all local branches: main for releases, develop for integration, many feature branches, fix branches, and two experiment branches.

36
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The star marks the branch I am on now.

37
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
The log graph shows the history.

38
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Each line is a commit, a saved snapshot with a message.

39
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
The lines with a slash show a feature branch splitting from develop and merging back.

40
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The names in brackets are branches.

41
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Each feature, such as nights column or ansible rollback, is visible as a unit because we merged with no fast forward.

42
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
A tag is a permanent label on one commit, used for releases.

43
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
We have version 1.0.0 and 1.1.0.

44
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
A branch moves forward as you commit, a tag stays put.

45
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
A remote is another copy of the repository on a server.

46
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Ours is called origin and points to GitHub.

47
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
The address contains no password.

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Local and remote are different things.

49
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
My local repository is on this laptop.

50
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
The remote is on GitHub.

51
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
So I ask GitHub directly.

52
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:46,000
It confirms a public repository, with nineteen branches and both release tags, and the develop branch is at the same commit locally and on GitHub.

53
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Issues and pull requests do not exist yet, only their templates and prepared texts.

54
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Finally, one commit in detail.

55
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:07,000
It has an identifier, an author, a date and a message that follows our convention: type, colon, short description.

56
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Here feat means a new feature.

57
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The statistics show exactly which files changed, here the my bookings template and a test.

58
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Small, focused commits make review and rollback easy.


